A Flutter widget test cannot do this. Widget tests only control Flutter’s in-process widget tree; the real iOS permission alert and delivered notification belong to SpringBoard and are inaccessible to `WidgetTester`.

Use an XCUITest/native UI test for this flow:

```swift
import XCTest

final class NotificationTests: XCTestCase {
    func testGrantsPermissionAndReceivesScheduledNotification() {
        let app = XCUIApplication()
        app.launchArguments += ["-schedule-test-notification"]
        app.launch()

        app.buttons["Enable notifications"].tap()

        let springboard = XCUIApplication(bundleIdentifier: "com.apple.springboard")
        let allowButton = springboard.alerts.buttons["Allow"]
        XCTAssertTrue(allowButton.waitForExistence(timeout: 5))
        allowButton.tap()

        // The app should schedule a short-delay local notification after permission
        // is granted and then move to the background.
        XCUIDevice.shared.press(.home)

        let notification = springboard
            .otherElements["NotificationCenter"]
            .staticTexts["Test notification"]

        XCTAssertTrue(
            notification.waitForExistence(timeout: 15),
            "The scheduled notification was not delivered"
        )
    }
}
```

The app must expose an accessibility-labelled `Enable notifications` button and, when launched with `-schedule-test-notification`, schedule a local notification shortly after authorization. Reset notification authorization between runs, since iOS normally shows the permission dialog only once per app installation.

Keep Flutter widget tests for mocked permission/scheduling behavior; run this real system-level assertion from the iOS UI-test target on a simulator or device.

