A Flutter widget test cannot tap the real iOS notification permission dialog or inspect Notification Center. Both are native UI outside Flutter’s test tree.

The reliable split is:

```dart
testWidgets('requests and schedules a notification after permission is granted',
    (tester) async {
  final notifications = FakeNotifications(permissionGranted: true);

  await tester.pumpWidget(
    MaterialApp(home: NotificationPage(notifications: notifications)),
  );

  await tester.tap(find.text('Enable notifications'));
  await tester.pump();

  expect(notifications.permissionRequested, isTrue);
  expect(notifications.scheduled, hasLength(1));
  expect(notifications.scheduled.single.title, 'Reminder');
});
```

This widget test verifies the observable Flutter behavior using a small fake. Verifying the real permission dialog and delivered notification requires an iOS integration/device test plus native UI automation such as XCUITest. Even an `integration_test` cannot tap the permission alert through Flutter finders or inspect Notification Center directly. Reset notification authorization between device runs so the system dialog appears consistently.
